
  [1mThe fork:[0m whether this change ships storage plumbing alone, or plumbing plus at least one real preference wired
  end-to-end into the page.

  - [1mPlumbing + one or two real preferences[0m — the module lands with actual callers. The obvious candidates given what
    exists: "remember my username" (prefill the field on return) and a light/dark theme toggle. Buys you a design
    validated against real usage, and a demonstrable "it persisted" you can see by reloading. Costs: touches [38;5;153mindex.html[39m
    and [38;5;153mapp.js[39m, not just new files.
  - [1mPlumbing only[0m — a generic [38;5;153mget[39m/[38;5;153mset[39m/[38;5;153mremove[39m preferences module with defaults and validation, no UI changes, no callers
    yet. Buys a smaller diff. Costs: the API gets designed against guesses, and nothing proves persistence works in the
    actual app.

  One thing I'll rule out regardless of your answer: [1mthe password never goes into storage.[0m "Remember me" means the
  username field only. Persisting a password in [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m puts it in plaintext, readable by any script on the origin,
  and I won't design that in.

  Cheap to change later: adding more preference keys. Expensive: the API's shape (whether callers get a typed accessor
  per preference or a generic string-keyed bag), which is easier to settle with one real caller in hand.

  [1mMy recommendation: plumbing plus both preferences.[0m They're small, they exercise different value types (boolean and
  string/enum), and they turn "persists across sessions" into something you can verify by reloading the page rather than
  by reading the code.
